    {
     "type": "quiz",
     "title": "Knowledge Check: Deduction vs Abduction",
     "question": "Holmes's leap from a callus and an ink stain to \"this man is a law clerk\" is best classified as which kind of reasoning?",
     "options": [
      {
       "text": "Abduction — an inference to the best available explanation, not a certainty",
       "correct": true
      },
      {
       "text": "Pure deduction from an established general rule",
       "correct": false
      },
      {
       "text": "Induction from a large sample of prior observations",
       "correct": false
      },
      {
       "text": "An unconstrained, random guess with no supporting logic",
       "correct": false
      }
     ],
     "explanation": "Holmes's conclusion follows a plausible but non-guaranteed line of reasoning built on background knowledge — Peirce's abduction, or inference to the best explanation. It is disciplined, not random, but it remains a hypothesis that new evidence could overturn, unlike true deduction's guaranteed conclusions.",
     "visual": {
      "type": "diagram",
      "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"60\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"34\">KNOWLEDGE CHECK</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"100\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"20\">Deduction versus abduction</text><rect x=\"150\" y=\"150\" width=\"800\" height=\"220\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"#0ea5e9\" fill-opacity=\"0.1\" stroke=\"#0ea5e9\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"235\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#0ea5e9\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"22\">The \"Science of Deduction\" is</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"280\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffffff\" font-size=\"17\">really abduction: inference to the best explanation</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"320\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"15\">Consider the full lesson before you answer</text><rect x=\"205\" y=\"420\" width=\"150\" height=\"70\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"#8b5cf6\" fill-opacity=\"0.1\" stroke=\"#8b5cf6\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"280\" y=\"465\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#8b5cf6\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"22\">A</text><rect x=\"385\" y=\"420\" width=\"150\" height=\"70\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"#8b5cf6\" fill-opacity=\"0.1\" stroke=\"#8b5cf6\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"460\" y=\"465\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#8b5cf6\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"22\">B</text><rect x=\"565\" y=\"420\" width=\"150\" height=\"70\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"#8b5cf6\" fill-opacity=\"0.1\" stroke=\"#8b5cf6\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"640\" y=\"465\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#8b5cf6\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"22\">C</text><rect x=\"745\" y=\"420\" width=\"150\" height=\"70\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"#8b5cf6\" fill-opacity=\"0.1\" stroke=\"#8b5cf6\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"820\" y=\"465\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#8b5cf6\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"22\">D</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"560\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"16\">Choose the single best explanation</text></svg>",
      "caption": "Testing whether you can spot true abduction"
     }
    },
    {
     "type": "application",
     "title": "Reasoning Like a Scientist, Not a Guesser",
     "content": "1. **Generate more than one hypothesis before you commit.** The instant you have an explanation that feels good enough, force yourself to name a second and third candidate. Premature closure is the single most common way abductive reasoning goes wrong.\n\n2. **Actively hunt for disconfirming evidence.** Don't just ask what would prove your leading hypothesis right; ask what fact, if you found it, would prove it wrong — then go look for that fact specifically.\n\n3. **Rank hypotheses by plausibility, not by order discovered.** Weigh each candidate against what you already know about how the world typically works, the same background knowledge Holmes leans on for every callus and stain.\n\n4. **Treat every conclusion as provisional.** State it to yourself as \"the best current explanation\" rather than \"the answer,\" so one new fact can update it without triggering defensiveness.\n\n5. **Write the reasoning chain down.** A visible trail — observation, candidate hypotheses, the evidence that favored one over the others — lets you or someone else audit exactly where you might have accepted a conclusion too quickly.\n\nTaken together, these five habits are what separate a disciplined abductive reasoner from someone who simply guesses confidently and hopes. Peirce never claimed abduction was infallible — only that it was the sole method capable of generating new ideas at all. Your job is not to eliminate the guessing, since guessing is the whole engine, but to make every guess accountable to evidence, revisable on demand, and never mistaken for the certainty that true deduction alone can provide.",
     "visual": {
      "type": "diagram",
      "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"60\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"34\">REASON LIKE A SCIENTIST</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"100\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"20\">Five habits for disciplined abduction</text><rect x=\"100\" y=\"175\" width=\"900\" height=\"90\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"#6366f1\" fill-opacity=\"0.12\" stroke=\"#6366f1\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"145\" y=\"230\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#6366f1\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"24\">1.</text><text x=\"195\" y=\"230\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#ffffff\" font-size=\"18\">Generate more than one hypothesis</text><rect x=\"100\" y=\"293\" width=\"900\" height=\"90\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"#8b5cf6\" fill-opacity=\"0.12\" stroke=\"#8b5cf6\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"145\" y=\"348\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#8b5cf6\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"24\">2.</text><text x=\"195\" y=\"348\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#ffffff\" font-size=\"18\">Hunt for disconfirming evidence</text><rect x=\"100\" y=\"411\" width=\"900\" height=\"90\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"#10b981\" fill-opacity=\"0.12\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"145\" y=\"466\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"24\">3.</text><text x=\"195\" y=\"466\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#ffffff\" font-size=\"18\">Rank hypotheses by plausibility</text><rect x=\"100\" y=\"529\" width=\"900\" height=\"90\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" fill-opacity=\"0.12\" stroke=\"#f59e0b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"145\" y=\"584\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"24\">4.</text><text x=\"195\" y=\"584\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#ffffff\" font-size=\"18\">Treat conclusions as provisional</text><rect x=\"100\" y=\"647\" width=\"900\" height=\"90\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"#ec4899\" fill-opacity=\"0.12\" stroke=\"#ec4899\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"145\" y=\"702\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#ec4899\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"24\">5.</text><text x=\"195\" y=\"702\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#ffffff\" font-size=\"18\">Write down your reasoning chain</text></svg>",
      "caption": "Five habits that turn guessing into disciplined abduction"
     }
    }
