     "type": "example",
     "title": "The Art Museum Study",
     "content": "One of the best-documented real-world tests of a Holmes-style curriculum doesn't come from a detective agency — it comes from medical school. Several medical schools, including Yale, have sent first- and second-year students to art museums specifically to practice systematic visual description: stand in front of an unfamiliar painting, describe only what is literally visible, resist jumping to interpretation, and build the description outward detail by detail before drawing any conclusion about the scene's meaning.\n\nThe transfer effect is the striking part. Students who completed this training showed measurable improvements in unrelated clinical observation tasks — describing dermatology photographs and radiology images more accurately and more completely than students who hadn't gone through the museum sessions. The skill being trained wasn't \"art appreciation\"; it was the raw, general discipline of describing before diagnosing, seeing before concluding, which is exactly the observation-before-hypothesis sequence covered throughout this book.\n\nSimilar structured-observation training shows up in some law-enforcement academies, using the same basic exercise: describe a scene exhaustively and neutrally before any theory of what happened is allowed to form. In both fields, the professional stakes of jumping to a premature conclusion are severe — a missed lesion, a contaminated crime scene — which is exactly why deliberately teaching the slower, observation-first habit has been worth the investment. It's real evidence for Konnikova's central claim: this is a trainable skill with measurable transfer, not a fixed talent some people are simply born with.",
